## INTRODUCTION
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), are a class of highly ordered porous materials constructed from various metal ions/clusters and organic ligands. These materials have attracted intense attention over last two decades.1-3 Compared with conventional porous materials such as zeolites and other carbon-based materials, MOFs promise a wider range of applications such as in luminescence, sensing, separation, catalysis, and gas storage. The diverse array of possible applications originate in the programmable structures, customizable chemical functionalities, versatile architectures, and relatively mild synthetic conditions that are hallmarks of the MOF material.4-9 However, despite the advantages of MOF materials, there has yet to be widespread utilization of this material in real world applications.
Stability to the conditions of utilization is the most important prerequisite to any envisaged applications.10-12 Particularly, the low stability toward water has considerably limited MOFs' practical application and commercialization. Water or moisture is usually present in most industrial processes, including carbon dioxide capture from flue gas,13,14 water desalination,15 proton conduction,16-18 moderate-temperature heat storage,19,20 and many catalytic processes.21-24 Unless a particular MOF is stable in these conditions it cannot
be utilized. For instance, one of the most well-studied MOFs, MOF-5,25 decomposes gradually in the presence of water vapor, restricting its applications under moisture rich conditions. MOFs with high stability have been always pursued among the scientific community, but they have been difficult to realize in practicality.
New, more stable examples of MOFs are produced every year with the aid of improved understanding of MOF stability. Reducing the contact between water molecules and MOF host frameworks, particularly the coordination bonds, was believed to be an effective strategy to enhance a MOF's moisture/water resistance.26-31 For instance, through introducing hydrophobic pore surfaces or blocked metal ions, water molecules are prevented from approaching the framework. Therefore, some MOFs have been specifically functionalized by introducing nonpolar alkyl functional, fluorinated, or methyl groups to sustain the framework robustness in the presence of aqueous medium.26-29,31 Moreover, covering hydrophobic polymers such as polydimethylsiloxane (PDMs), on the surface of MOF particles can also effectively enhance the water resistance.30 In addition to enhancing the hydrophobicity of MOF structure, another common strategy is to increase the M–L bond strength. In recent years, many stable MOFs have been constructed with specific nitrogen-based ligands or by the combination of highly charged metal ions with carboxylate-
based ligands. This approach can be rationalized by the hard/soft acid/base (HSAB) principle.\(^{32-41}\) Examples of highly stable MOFs include zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs),\(^{37}\) Materials Institute Lavoisier (MILs),\(^{36}\) pyrazolate-based frameworks,\(^{33,37,41}\) and \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\)-based carboxylate MOFs.\(^{32,39,40,45}\) A representative example of a \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\)-based carboxylate MOF is UiO-66. UiO-66 is constructed from \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) clusters and 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate \((\mathrm{BDC}^{2-})\) ligands.\(^{15,32}\) It is well known for its excellent thermal, mechanical, and chemical stability in water as well as strongly acidic and weakly basic aqueous solutions. The stability of UiO-66 can be attributed to the strong interaction between the \(Z\mathrm{r}^{\mathrm{IV}}\) metal centers and the chelating carboxylate O donors. However, not all of the \(Z\mathbf{r}\)-MOFs demonstrated the same stability. The water stability of some \(Z\mathbf{r}\)-MOFs has been challenged. For example, UiO-67,\(^{32,43}\) an isoreticular version of UiO-66, has been found unstable toward water.\(^{44,46}\) The lack of stability was mainly attributed to its larger cavity, and/or the solvent-framework intermolecular forces. It should be pointed out that to date MOFs with high stability typically rely upon employing robust
M–L coordination bond in the framework. However, a divergent approach, the role of organic ligands in stability, particularly their rigidity/flexibility, is rarely studied.
In this work, the investigation of the role of the organic linkers in the framework on stability is addressed. A series of three ligand-rotamers with the conserved connectivity but different rigidity were rationally designed and synthesized. Constructed from \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH}_{4})(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{3}\) (\(\mathfrak{n}=8\) or 12) secondary building units (hereafter referred to as \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\)), thirteen \(Z\mathbf{r}\)-MOFs were obtained with the linkers in our study. The resulting stabilities of the structures were intensively studied and compared. Finally, it is demonstrated that ligand-rigidification enhances the stability of MOFs towards water/moisture.
Firstly, by using the \(\mathrm{L_{A}}\) type ligands \((\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1},\ \mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}\), and \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{3}\), four peripheral naphthoate arms with a varying core), as depicted in Figure 1, we successfully constructed six different \(Z\mathbf{r}\)-MOFs, referred to as \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}\) -flu (1), \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}\) -csq (2), \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}\) - \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}\) -shp (3), \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{12}\) -shp (4), \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}\) -csq (5, also known as NU-1003\(^{47}\)), and \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{3}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}\) -flu (6), respectively. In this naming
scheme, \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}^{1}\), \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}^{2}\), or \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}^{3}\) are the name of the ligands, the \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}^{8}\) and \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}^{12}\) stands for how many ligands are coordinated to the \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\) cluster, and the last three letters are the topology of the framework structures. By replacing naphthyl groups in \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}\) type ligands with phenyl groups, corresponding benzoate-based ligands \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}^{1}\), \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}^{2}\), and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}^{3}\) have also been synthesized (Figure 2). \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}^{1}\)-\(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}^{8}\)-\(\mathrm{flu}\) (7), \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}^{1}\)-\(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}^{8}\)-\(\mathrm{csq}\) (8, also known as NU-1000\(^{48}\)), \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}^{2}\)-\(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}^{8}\)-\(\mathrm{scu}\) (9, also known as NU-901\(^{49}\)), and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}^{3}\)-\(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}^{8}\)-\(\mathrm{flu}\) (10) were then constructed with the ligands and 8-connected \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\) clusters, respectively. Each of these MOFs were tested and compared for differences in connectivity, structure, and ligand rigidity/flexibility. Control experiments were conducted to assess the influence of each variation in the ligand on the stability of the corresponding MOFs. It was found that the rigidity of the ligands was the most important of the characteristics tested for enhancing the stability of the MOFs. In this work, it was determined that topology and porosity are not determinative factors for stability in the system. Finally, the validity of this strategy was further verified by the examination on three Zr-MOFs, \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}^{1}\)-\(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}^{12}\)-\(\mathrm{fcu}\)
(11, also known as UiO-67), \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}^{2}\)-\(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}^{12}\)-\(\mathrm{fcu}\) (12), and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}^{3}\)-\(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}^{8}\)-\(\mathrm{bcu}\) (13, also known as PCN-700-\(\mathrm{Me}_{4}^{50}\)). These MOFs were constructed from \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}\) type ditopic ligands (Figure 4). Again, the stability of \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}^{2}\)-\(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}^{12}\)-\(\mathrm{fcu}\) (12) and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}^{3}\)-\(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}^{8}\)-\(\mathrm{bcu}\) (13) structures with rigidified ligands was dramatically enhanced compared to that of \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}^{1}\)-\(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}^{12}\)-\(\mathrm{fcu}\) (11), which had more flexible biphenyl\(^{4,4'}\)-dicarboxylate ligands in the structure. These systematic studies suggest that ligand-rigidification is an efficient and general approach for enhancing the stability of Zr-MOFs.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Ligands  Regulated  Structure  Variation.  Nine  ligands, namely \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}\) , \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}\) , \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{3}\) , \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}^{1}\) , \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}^{2}\) , \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{3}\) , \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{1}\) , \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{2}\) , and \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{3}\) have been used to construct a series of \(Z\mathrm{r}\) -MOFs. \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{\mathrm{n}}\) and \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{\mathrm{n}}\) \({\mathfrak{n}}=1\) , 2, and 3) are tetracarboxylate ligands, and \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{\mathrm{n}}\) \({\mathfrak{n}}=1\) , 2, and 3) are dicarboxylate ligands. Each of the ligands consist of a central core and several peripheral arms. The peripheral arms for \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{\mathrm{n}}\) and \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{\mathrm{n}}\) are  4  naphthoates  and  4  benzoates, respectively, and those of \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{\mathrm{n}}\) are 2 carboxylates. The cores for \(\mathrm{L_{X}}^{1}\) \(\mathrm{~\textit~{~X~}~}=\mathrm{~\textit~{~A~}~}\) , B, and C) are biphenyl groups. The core
component for the \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{2}\) ligands is the pyrene, and those for \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{3}\) are methylate biphenyl \((2,2^{\prime},4,4^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}\)-hexamethylbiphenyl for \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{3}\) and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{3}\), and \(2,2^{\prime},6^{\prime}\)-tetramethylbiphenyl for \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{3}\)). Due to these differences, it is expected that these ligands would generate structures with very different structural rigidity/flexibility characteristics. A survey of known single crystal structures in the Cambridge Structural Database (CSD, update of Feb. 2018) containing biphenyl, \(2,2^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}\)-tetramethylbiphenyl, 2,6-dimethylbiphenyl, or 1-phenylpyrene fragment was performed. A statistics of dihedral angles between two neighboring aromatic rings \((\theta_{1}\) to \(\theta_{4})\) in these structures is shown in Figure 3. The values of \(\theta_{1}\) for biphenyl ranges from 0 to \(90^{\circ}\), although only 17 structures out of 6427 reported structures have \(\theta_{1}\) angles between \(80^{\circ}-90^{\circ}\). The wide distribution of \(\theta_{1}\) indicates that the two phenyl rings in a biphenyl entity readily rotate along the \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C}\) bond between them, and the energy barrier for the rotation of one phenyl ring with respect to the other is low. In contrast, the values of \(\theta_{2}\) in 34 structures of the reported structures containing \(2,2^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}\)-tetramethylbiphenyl are in the range of 73 to \(90^{\circ}\). Over half of the structures reported with this ligand core show \(\theta_{1}\) angles higher than \(85^{\circ}\). It is suggested that the spatial orientation of the two aromatic rings in \(2,2^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}\)-tetramethylbiphenyl is strongly constrained due to the steric hindrances of the neighboring methyl groups. The rotation of one ring, with respect to the other, to deviate from the ideal configuration of \(\theta_{2}=90^{\circ}\) is energetically unfavorable. The values of \(\theta_{3}\,(57-90^{\circ})\) and \(\theta_{4}\,(41-85^{\circ})\) imply that the rotation of one aromatic ring with respect to the other in 2,6-dimethylbiphenyl or 1-phenylpyrene is also constrained, but not as strongly constrained as in \(2,2^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}\)-tetramethylbiphenyl. These statistics clearly indicate that an ordering for the flexibility of those structural motifs should be: biphenyl \(>1\)-phenylpyrene \(>2,6\)-dimethylbiphenyl \(>2,2^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}\)-tetramethylbiphenyl. It can be consequently expected that the flexibility of the ligands used in this work follows the same ordering as their backbone cores: \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{1}>\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{2}>\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{3}\). A series of \(\mathrm{Zr}\)-MOFs were then constructed by these ligands. Although the coordination bonds between \(\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}\) and carboxylate O atom are strong, many reported \(\mathrm{Zr}\)-MOFs have been shown to be unstable,\(^{43,46}\) implying that strong metal-ligand coordination bonds in MOFs are a necessary condition for stability, but not a sufficient condition for high stability. Thus, the investigation of the stability of \(\mathrm{Zr}\)-MOFs built from these ligands with quite different rigidity/flexibility may offer insight to understand the determinative factors of their stability and further to guide the design of highly stable MOFs.
Five \(\mathrm{Zr}\)-MOFs were obtained from the \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{1}\) type ligands. The MOFs \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{12}{-}s h p\) (3), \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (2), and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (1) were isolated from the solvothermal reactions between \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}\) and \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) with different reaction conditions (see experimental section for details). The as synthesized MOFs have a (4, 12)-connected framework with the \(s h p\) topology constructed from 12-connected \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters and square-planar tetratopic \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}\) ligands. Similarly, a (4, 8)-connected framework with the \(c s q\) topology constructed from 8-connected \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters and square-planar \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}\) ligands, and a (4, 8)-connected framework with the \(f l u\) topology constructed by 8-connected
\(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters and tetrahedral \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}\) ligands, were also synthesized. \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (7) was synthesized from the \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{1}\) ligand which is isoreticular to \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (1) with a slightly smaller unit cell. The framework of \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{12}{-}f c u\) (11) is a 12-connected framework with the \(f c u\) topology. This system is constructed from 12-connected \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters and linear \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{1}\) ligands.
Another five \(\mathrm{Zr}\)-MOFs were obtained from the \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{2}\) type ligands. \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (5) and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{12}{-}s h p\) (4) were synthesized from \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2}\). The resulting structures are
isoreticular to \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{1}{-}c s q\) (2) and \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{12}{-}s h p\) (3), respectively. \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (8) and \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}s c u\) (9) were synthesized from \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{2}\) . The \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (8) is isoreticular to \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (2) and \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (5). The framework of \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}\) - \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}s c u\) (9) is constructed by 8-connected \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters and square-planar \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}\) ligands. This structure can be regarded as a (4, 8)-connected framework with the scu topology. \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{12}.\) - fcu (12) was synthesized from ligands \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{2}\) , and it is isoreticular to \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{12}{-}f c u\) (11).
Lastly, three \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs were obtained from the \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{3}\) type ligands. \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{3}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (6) and \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{3}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (10), synthesized from \(\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{L}}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{3}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{L}}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{3}\) , respectively, are both isoreticular to \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (1). \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{3}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}b c u\) (13) was synthesized from \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{3}\) , and has a (4,8)-connected network with the bcu topology. This MOF is constructed from linear \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{3}\) ligands and 8-connected \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters. Additional structural details and structure descriptions for the newly synthesized \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs can be found in Section S7 of the Supporting Information.
Permanent Porosity. A series of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption measurements was used to confirm the permanent porosity of the thirteen \(Z\mathbf{r}\) - MOFs. After the solvent molecules (DMF and water) were removed through acetone solvent exchange and subsequent evacuation under vacuum, \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms were recorded at \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\) for \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (1), \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (2), \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}\) - \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{12}{-}s h p\) (3), \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{12}{-}s h p\) (4), \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (5), \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{3}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (6), \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (7), \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (8), \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}s c u\) (9), \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{3}\) - \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (10), \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{12}{-}f c u\) (11), \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{12}{-}f c u\) (12), and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{3}\) - \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}b c u\) (13), (Section 9 in Supporting Information). Saturated \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) uptakes of 850, 1410, 605, 1290, 575, 590, 585, 910, 620, 580, 595, 395, and \(350\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (STP) were achieved, respectively. The adsorption/desorption isotherms of the ten MOFs with the flu, shp, scu, fcu, and bcu topologies show type
I isotherms, indicating the presence of micropores in their structures. Meanwhile, stepwise type IV \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption/desorption isotherms were observed for \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}.\) - csq (2), \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (5) and \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (8), which implies a mesoporous structure. The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption results were used to confirm the permanent porosities of the thirteen MOFs after the removal of guest molecules.
Chemical Stability. The chemical stability of the \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs with tetratopic ligands under a range of conditions was tested. Herein, control experiments were conducted to assess the influence of each functionality on the stability of corresponding MOF.
With the same ligand and connectivity, but different structures, two sets of MOFs, \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (1) and \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (2), and \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (8) and \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}s c u\) (9), were studied and compared. As depicted in Table 1 and Figure S22, unlike most reported \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs, it was found that \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (1) is unstable in water. The intensity and position of the Powder X-Ray Diffraction (PXRD) peaks have obviously changed, indicating a significant change in the crystallinity of the bulk phase. Also collaborating this finding, the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption measurements have been almost completely lost after the water treatment. Combined, this would indicate the collapse of the framework. A similar situation demonstrating a low stability toward water for \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (2) was observed and confirmed by PXRD and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption measurements (Figure S23 and S36). Transversely, both \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (8) and \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}\) - \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{}^{8}{-}s c u\) (9) display high chemical stability.48,49 As shown in Table 1, Figure S23 and S36, they remain intact after immersion in water, acidic solution \((\mathrm{pH}=1)\) ), or weakly basic \((\mathrm{pH}~=~11)\) ) aqueous solutions, respectively. In our MOF system, the \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs built from the same ligand but with different structures could show similar stability, suggesting that the flexibility of ligand is the main impact factors that dictates the stability of these \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs.
Finally, the stability of the \( Z\mathbf{r} \)-MOFs constructed from different ligands but sharing the same topology, such as \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (1) and \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{3} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (6), \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12} \)-shp (3) and \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12} \)-shp (4), \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-cq (2) and \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-cq (5), or \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{1} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (7) and \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{3} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (10), were compared against their sister topology frameworks respectively. In each set, the two ligands under study contained different amounts of rigidities/flexibilities, yielding differences in the stability of the resulting framework. The \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (1) framework was found to be unstable when the sample was exposed to water, while the \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{3} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (6) framework remained intact after a variety of treatments in aqueous solution. After the \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{3} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (6) sample was soaked in different solutions from 2 M HCl to \( \mathrm{pH}=11 \) NaOH aqueous solution, the PXRD pattern showed no loss in crystallinity and the \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) sorption isotherms were unaltered (Figure S27 and S40). These results suggest that \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{3} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (6) is highly chemically stability. Similar stability difference were also found in other three sets of MOFs, \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12} \)-shp (3) and \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12} \)-shp (4), \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-cq (2) and \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-cq (5), and \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{1} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (7) and \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{3} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (10). As found to be unstable when the sample was exposed to water, while the \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-cq (2) (3) collapsed after water treatment, while the samples of \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12} \)-shp (4) and \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-cq (5) retained their crystallinity after immersion in water, HCl \( \mathrm{pH}=0 \), and NaOH solutions \( \mathrm{pH}=11 \) for \( 24~\mathrm{h} \), respectively (Figure S18 and S19). The \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{3} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (10) framework also has obvious differences from the \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{3} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (7) framework in terms of chemical stability. A comparison of PXRD patterns for both \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{1} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (7) and \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{3} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (10) after immersion in water, supported that the \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{3} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (10) framework is more stable than the \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{1} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (7) framework (Figure S28, 31, 41 and
S44). Meanwhile, the \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) sorption measurements for the \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{3} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8} \)-flu (10) sample were almost the same as that of the original sample after chemical stability testing (from \( \mathrm{pH}=11 \) NaOH to 2 M HCl aqueous solution). These systematic results demonstrate that the rigidification of the ligands through restricting the rotation of the core entities have remarkable implications for the stability of the corresponding MOFs.
As discussed above, it has been demonstrated that the ligand rigidification strategy can be used to stabilize the \( Z\mathbf{r} \)-MOFs constructed with tetratopic carboxylate ligands. \( Z\mathbf{r} \)-MOFs constructed from ditopic carboxylate ligands were also investigated to demonstrate the universality of the ligand rigidification strategy.
The chemical stability of \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{1} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12} \)-fcu (11), a ditopic carboxylate ligand based \( Z\mathbf{r} \)-MOF, has always been challenged.43-45 After immersion of \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{1} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12} \)-fcu (11) in water for \( 24~\mathrm{h} \) it was found that the PXRD patterns and \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) sorption measurements of the framework remained almost entirely unchanged (Table 1, Figure S32 and S45). However, the PXRD pattern and \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) sorption measurements for the framework rapidly changed after treatment with a weakly acidic aqueous solution \( \mathrm{pH}<4 \) or a base aqueous solution \( \mathrm{pH}>9 \). These results signify the framework's chemical stability weaknesses under such conditions, restricting its further application under harsher constraints.
Given our results with the tetratopic carboxylate ligands, it was speculated that the ligand-rigidification strategy may be applicable for the enhancement of the chemical stability of \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{1} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12} \)-fcu (11). To test this hypothesis, \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{2} \)-\( Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12} \)-fcu (12) was constructed by utilizing ligand \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{2} \). In comparison to \( \mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{1} \)-
\(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}\) -fcu (11), the effective pore and window sizes are reduced in \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}\) -fcu (12) owing to the larger dimension of the extended ligand \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{2}\) . Thus, the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption measurement for \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}\) -fcu (12) were slightly different to that of \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}.\) - fcu (11) (Figure S46). Noticeably, the chemical stability tests and subsequent PXRD profiles (Figure S33) and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption measurements (Figure S46) have demonstrated that the \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{2}\) - \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}\) -fcu (12) framework not only is stable to aqueous solutions, but the framework remains intact in strongly acidic solutions \(\left(6\mathrm{~M~HCl}\right)\) as well as weakly basic solutions \(\mathrm{pH}=\) 11). These results further confirmed that the incorporation of rigidified ligands into a MOF framework contributes to the stabilization of the MOF.
Additionally, the chemical stability of \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{3}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}\) -bcu (13) was also enhanced in comparison to \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}\) -fcu (11), although with reduced connectivity (Table 1, Figure S23 and S24). It was found that the \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{3}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}\) -bcu (13) also retains high porosity after treated with water, strong acid \(\left(12\mathrm{~M~HCl}\right)\) , and weak base \(\mathrm{pH}=11\) ), respectively. Clearly, both \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}\) -fcu (12) and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{3}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}\) -bcu (13) show higher stability toward aqueous solutions in a wide \(\mathrm{pH}\) rang than the parent MOF, \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}\) -fcu (11). This higher stability can be attributed not only to the reduced pore size and porosity of the isoreticular structures, but also to the restricted rotation of the ligand. It is worth noting that another UiO-67-type MOF with enhanced water stability was reported by Liellurd's group.46 It was found that UiO-67-Me, constructed by \(3,3"\) -position methylated biphenyl dicarboxylate ligands without the restriction of the flexibility of biphenyl backbone, gave a limited enhanced water stability to the UiO-67-Me framework.46 In contrast, the UiO-67-BN framework, with naphthyl functionalized ligands disturbed the free rotation of the biphenyl core. This change in the UiO-67-BN structure in comparison to the parent MOF or UiO-67-Me provided UiO-67-BN a higher chemical stability under the same conditions.46 These results imply that the hydrophobic groups in the ligand can lead to enhanced stability in the structure, but the rigidification of the ligand has a greater influence on the chemical stability of the MOF.
Proposed Mechanism for Stability Enhancement. The thermodynamic reason, such as the enhanced bond strength is believed to mainly dictate the framework or bond stability of MOFs. In this work, however we propose that it is possible to enhance the stability of a MOF through ligand-rigidification. Herein, we defined the flexibility of ligands as the rotational freedom of their neighboring aromatic rings. It may be roughly quantified by the energy barrier of rotating C-C bonds between two neighboring aromatic rings. Rigid linkers have higher energy barrier and tend to keep their conformation in MOFs (both pristine or decomposed states). The approach of a ligand-rigidification mechanism can mainly be explained from a kinetics perspective. In terms of the kinetics, a successive substitution reaction is generally considered to be the main contributor to MOF decomposition in the presence of water. In this process, defects emerge through the replacement of coordination bonds between the ligand and metal cluster with water molecules that become bound to the metal clusters. The backbones of rigid ligands such as \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2}\) , \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{3}\) , \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{2}\) , \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{3}\) , \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{2}\) , and
\(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{3}\) can be viewed as whole pieces, while flexible ligands such as \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}\) , \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{1}\) , and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{1}\) have higher degrees of rotational freedom due to their rotatable biphenyl cores. When the dissociation through water substitution occurs on one side of a ligand bound to the metal cluster, the ligand must have the rotational freedom to leave the original coordination sites. In the case of rigid ligands, the structure deformation is restricted by other coordination sites, thus the ligand does not move too far from its original position in the framework. Therefore, the lability of the M–L coordination bond at a particular dissociated sites can lead to rapid structural repair. This self-repair effect is more effective for ligands with higher connectivity because these ligands can tolerate the displacements of more coordination sites than a ligand with lower connectivity. This "three-dimensional (3D) chelating effect" has been described in our previous work.51 Overall, when high connectivity is exhibited for the ligand and metal cluster, dissociation due to water treatment does not result in structural collapse. However, for flexible ligands, the dissociation of the bond between a metal cluster and a ligand can happen without disturbing other coordination sites resulting in a slower and more difficult L-M bond reformation. As a result, defect ratio in the system will increase, further leading to the collapse of the framework. Furthermore, as depicted in Figure 1c and 2b, introduction of hexamethyl/tetramethyl into specific positions can latch the tetraploid/deficient ligand \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{3}\) , \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{3}\) and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{3}\) , in which the latching effect is more evident compared with \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2}\) , \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{2}\) and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{2}\) , resulting in MOFs with superior stability.
Water Adsorption. The ability to synthesize highly stable Zr-MOFs by utilizing the rigid ligand approach allows for further exploration of potential applications for these materials. Water adsorption/desorption performance in MOFs is an important consideration for the implementation of these materials for practical applications.52-58 High water uptake capacity and good cycling performance of the materials are both required for water capture applications. Water adsorption/desorption processes require not only chemical stability but also the mechanical stability of the adsorbents. The rigidified Zr-MOFs in this work that have shown high chemical stability in the presence of water have thus been further examined for their water adsorption/desorption performances (Section S13).
For comparison, the water uptake properties of \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}\) -flu (1), \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}\) -csq (2), \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}\) -shp (3), and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}\) -flu (7) were first studied. These MOFs were all synthesized from the biphenyl-based ligands \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}\) and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{1}\) , whose water isotherms suggest collapse of structures during the first round of desorption (Figure S48). This was verified by the water sorption experiments in subsequent cycles where the adsorption was greatly reduced. The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption measurements for the recovered MOF materials also showed signs of MOF decomposition, indicating the collapse of the structure after water adsorption. These results are consistent with the known low chemical stability of these MOFs.
The water adsorption properties of \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}\) -csq (5), \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2}\) - \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}\) -shp (4), \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}\) -csq (8), \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}\) -scu (9), and \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}\) - fcu (11), were then taken and the results were compared to the data from \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}\) -flu (1), \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}\) -csq (2), \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}\) -shp (3),
and \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (7), respectively. The water sorption for \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}.\) - \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}\) -csq (5) begins at \(P/P_{0}=0.7\) and reaches the maximum of \(1800\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (corresponding to a \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) accessible pore volume of \(1.45\;\mathrm{cc}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , where the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) accessible pore volume is \(1.81\;\mathrm{cc}\) \(\mathrm{g}^{-1},\) ) in the first cycle (Figure S51). The well-defined water isotherm and corresponding high water sorption indicate the enhanced stability of \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (5) toward water relative to the corresponding data for \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (2). However, less than half of the maximum water sorption \((850~\mathrm{\cm}^{3}~\mathrm{\g}^{-1})\) occurred under identical conditions in the second adsorption cycle for \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (5). The results of water uptake and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption experiment on the material demonstrate a decrease in the porosity. These results indicate that after the first cycle there was some decomposition of the material from the water adsorption experiment (Figure S49). A similar phenomenon was observed in \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}{-}s h p\) (4), \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (8), \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}.\) - scu (9), and \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}{-}f c u\) (11). The maximum amount of water adsorbed of these four materials was 940, 1590, 920, and 440 \(\mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively. A significant decrease in the amount of water adsorbed from the first to the second cycle (710, 610, 390, and \(210~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) ) was also observed for these materials (Figure  S49).  After  several  cycles,  the  water  adsorption capacities of the four MOFs were successively decreased, suggesting that their respective structures undergo partial decomposition during the water adsorption/desorption process. The  instability  of  these  frameworks  during  water adsorption/desorption  cycling  could  be  attributed  to  the capillary-force-driven  pore  collapse.  This  suggests  that causatively from the evacuation of water from the framework, rather than a competitive hydrolytic instability based with water, the pores within the framework collapse.45
Finally, we found that compared to the other frameworks in this study, \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{3}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (6), \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{3}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (10), \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}{-}f c u\) (12), and \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{3}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}b c u\) (13) exhibit superior water uptake performance. The water sorption isotherm for \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{3}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (6) shows obvious steps with a hysteresis loop (Figure S50). The limited water uptake at lower pressure for this material indicates that the affinity of the water molecules to the MOF surface is weak. Therefore, a higher water vapor pressure is required to induce pore filling. Once the pore filling begins, at \(P/P_{0}=0.70\) , maximum sorption is quickly reached with a total capacity of \(920~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(P/P_{0}=0.9\) . Similarly to the water isotherm of \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{3}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (6), the water adsorption of \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{3}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}.\) - f lu (10) demonstrated an isotherm with a steep adsorption curve that began at \(P/P_{0}{=}\,0.45\) and reached \(1250~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(P/P_{0}=0.80\) (Figure S50). The water isotherm of \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}{-}f c u\) (12) (Figure 51) has a sigmoidal shape with a moderate hysteresis loop at \(P/P_{0}=0.40\mathrm{~-~}0.50\) . For \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}{-}f c u\) (12), the maximum water sorption was \(550~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (at \(298\ \mathrm{K}\) ) at \(P/P_{0}\,{=}\,0.90\) . For \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{3}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}b c u\) (13), the water sorption began at \(P/P_{0}=0.65\) and reached a maximum water sorption of 385 \(\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(P/P_{0}=0.85\) . Cycle performance for water sorption experiments for \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{3}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (6), \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{3}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (10), \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}.\) - fcu (12), and \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{3}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}b c u\) (13) were also conducted (Figure S50). The cycling experiments indicated that these four MOFs retain most of the cycling performance integrity between the first and last water sorption cycle. It should be noted that the \(\mathrm{L_{C}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}{-}f c u\) (12) framework showed a high mechanical stability in the water adsorption/desorption processes. The mechanical stability of this MOF was shared with other MOFs built from methylate cores \((\mathrm{L}_{x}3)\) . However, the MOFs built from pyrene cores, \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (5), \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}{-}s h p\) (4), \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}.\) -
\(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}\) -csq (8) and \(\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r_{8}}^{8}{-}s c u\) (9) did not share the same mechanical stability. The difference in the mechanical stability for these two groups of MOFs is not unexpected as the \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}\) and \({\mathrm{L}}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{2}\) ligands are more flexible than the \({\mathrm{L}}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{2}\) ligand due to the extra rotational freedom between the peripheral phenyl arms and the central cores in the \(\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}\) and \({\mathrm{L}}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{2}\) ligands ( \(\theta_{4}\) in Figure 3d). Clearly, the high cycling performance in the water adsorption/desorption performance for these \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs can be attributed to the rigidity of their corresponding ligands.
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, thirteen 3D \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs with distinct framework topologies and varied porosities have been constructed. The frameworks consisted of a variety of \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\) clusters and three classes of ligands, each with different degrees of flexibility, \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{1}>\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{2}>\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{3}\) . The results of the study determined that the five \(\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{1}\) ligand based \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs showed less stability than the other \({\mathrm{L}}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{2}\) and \({\mathrm{L}}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{3}\) ligand based \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs. This was confirmed by a comparison of PXRD patterns and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption experiments from before and after water treatment experiments under a dynamic range of \(\mathrm{pH}\) . Moreover, water cycling adsorption/desorption experiments revealed that the thirteen \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs showed a significant difference not only in chemical stability but also in mechanical stability. All of the \({\mathrm{L}}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{1}\) ligand-based MOFs lost most of their water sorption performance during the second cycle of water sorption. The water sorption performance of the \({\mathrm{L}}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{2}\) ligand-based MOFs gradually decreased in successive water sorption cycles. In contrast to the \({\mathrm{L}}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{1}\) and \({\mathrm{L}}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{2}\) ligand-based MOFs, the performance of the \({\mathrm{L}}_{\mathrm{X}}{}^{3}\) ligand-based MOFs was almost completely conserved from cycle to cycle. These results can be correlated to the differences in the flexibility of the ligands, and simultaneously suggest a useful ligand rigidification strategy to obtain highly porous and stable (chemically or mechanically) MOFs.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
General Information. Commercially sourced chemicals were used as purchased without further purification. A detailed list of chemical sources can be found in the Supporting Information Section 1.
Instruments. The PXRD patterns were recorded on a Rigaku Smartlab3 X-ray Powder Diffractometer equipped with a Cu sealed tube \(\mathcal{R}\,=\,1.54178\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) ). Simulations of the PXRD patterns were carried out by the single-crystal data and diffraction-crystal module on Mercury. The Mercury program is available free of charge at https://www.ccdc.cam.ac.uk/. \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and water vapor sorption experiments were conducted using a Micrometritics ASAP 2020 system at 77 and \(298~\mathrm{~K~}\) , respectively. All \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR data sets were collected on a Mercury \(400~\mathrm{\:MHz}\) NMR spectrometer. All FT-IR spectra were recorded on an IR Affinity-1 instrument. TGA data were obtained on a TGA-50 (SHIMADZU) thermogravimetric analyzer with a heating rate of \(10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C\min^{-1}}\) under nitrogen atmosphere. ICP were performed by a PerkinElmer Optima 8000 optical emission spectrometer. Elemental analysis (EA) was performed by vario EL cube Elementar.
Synthesis of ligands. A detailed procedure for the synthesis of \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}\) , \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{2}\) , \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{3}\) , \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{1}\) , \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{2}\) , and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{2}\) can be found in the Supporting Information under Section 2.
Synthesis of reported MOFs. NU-1103 \((\mathrm{L_{A}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (5)), NU-1000 \((\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r_{6}}^{8}{-}c s q\) (8)), NU-901 \((\mathrm{L_{B}}^{2}{-}Z\mathrm{r_{6}}^{8}{-}s c u\) (9)), UiO-67 \((\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{1}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{12}{-}f c u\) (11)) and PCN-700-Me4 \((\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{C}}{}^{3}{-}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}b c u\) (13)) were prepared according to reported methods.
Synthesis of \(\mathbf{L_{A}}^{1}{-}Z\mathbf{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}f l u\) (1, BUT-91): A solution of \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}\) ( \(25~\mathrm{mg}\) , \(0.028\ \mathrm{mmol})\) ), \(Z\mathrm{r}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}\) ( \(100\;\mathrm{mg},\,0.56\;\mathrm{mmol})\) , and formic acid \(\mathrm{[0.3~mL}\) , \(88\%\) ) in \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) of DMF was generated and sealed in a \(20~\mathrm{mL}\) glass vial. Then the vial was placed into an oven and heated to \(120\ \,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 48 hours followed by cooling to \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a rate of \(0.5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\) minute. The resulting colorless crystals were collected by suction filtration and washed with DMF \(\mathrm{[50~mL)}\) and acetone \(\mathrm{[50~mL)}\) , in turn, for three times at room temperature, and then dried in air \((20\;\mathrm{mg})\) .
Synthesis of \(\mathbf{L_{A}}^{1}{-}Z\mathbf{r}_{6}{}^{8}{-}c s q\) (2, BUT-92): A solution of \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}\) ( \(5~\mathrm{mg}\) , 0.0057 mmol), \(Z\mathrm{r}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}\) ( \(3~\mathrm{mg}\) , 0.013 mmol), and formic acid \(\mathrm{[0.25~mL}\) , \(98\%\) ) in \(2~\mathrm{mL}\) of DMF was generated and sealed in a \(5~\mathrm{mL}\) glass vial. Then the vial was placed into an oven and heated to \(100\ \,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 12 hours followed by cooling to \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a rate of \(0.5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\) minute. The resulting colorless crystals were collected by suction filtration, washed with DMF \(\mathrm{[50~mL)}\) and acetone \(\mathrm{[50~mL)}\) , in turn, for three times at room temperature, and then dried in air \((3.5\;\mathrm{mg})\) .
Synthesis of \(\mathbf{L_{A}}^{1}{-}Z\mathbf{r}_{12}{-}s h p\) (3, BUT-93): A solution of \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{A}}{}^{1}\) (5 mg, 0.006 mmol), \(Z\mathrm{r}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}\) (3 mg, 0.013 mmol), and formic acid \(\mathrm{[0.35~mL}\) , \(88\%\) ) in \(2~\mathrm{mL}\) of DMF was generated and sealed in a \(5~\mathrm{mL}\) glass vial. Then the vial was placed into an oven and heated to \(100\ \,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 12 hours followed by cooling to \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a rate of \(0.5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\) minute. The resulting colorless crystals were collected by suction filtration, washed with DMF \(\mathrm{[50~mL)}\) and acetone \(\mathrm{[50~mL)}\) , in turn, for three times at room temperature, and then dried in air \((4.0\;\mathrm{mg})\) .
